1
00:03:02,283 --> 00:03:04,015
Bang.

2
00:03:10,758 --> 00:03:13,569
Oh. This is a great office.

3
00:03:13,593 --> 00:03:16,005
Well, it will be
when I get it fixed up.

4
00:03:16,029 --> 00:03:19,131
Uh. You're still
tilting to the left, I see.

5
00:03:21,635 --> 00:03:23,079
There.

6
00:03:23,103 --> 00:03:25,282
I bet you a quarter
it's still not straight.

7
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:27,750
I couldn't have done
better if I'd used a level.

8
00:03:27,774 --> 00:03:30,553
Design workshop.
Best class at Smith.

9
00:03:30,577 --> 00:03:34,224
It was the only A I got
the whole semester.

10
00:03:34,248 --> 00:03:37,694
You, uh, going out of town...

11
00:03:37,718 --> 00:03:39,984
or leaving Clay Graham again?

12
00:03:41,054 --> 00:03:43,232
For good this time.

13
00:03:43,256 --> 00:03:44,355
I hope so.

14
00:03:45,525 --> 00:03:47,770
You'll stay with
me. I insist. No, I...

15
00:03:47,794 --> 00:03:50,340
No, I have a lovely new
apartment and I need help fix...

16
00:03:50,364 --> 00:03:53,076
I was thinking that I
should go to a hotel.

17
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:56,246
Andrea, you were
my favorite roommate.

18
00:03:56,270 --> 00:03:59,082
So it's settled.

19
00:03:59,106 --> 00:04:01,218
Good. Because you know what?

20
00:04:01,242 --> 00:04:03,442
I already sent the trunk over.

21
00:04:09,650 --> 00:04:12,495
She'll be back. Unless
she finds someone else.

22
00:04:12,519 --> 00:04:14,730
You know as well as I
do she's out there looking.

23
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:18,268
Even as we speak.

24
00:04:18,292 --> 00:04:21,437
I'd call her, Clay. Yeah?

25
00:04:21,461 --> 00:04:22,772
If you want Andrea back,

26
00:04:22,796 --> 00:04:24,307
you'd better do
something about it.

27
00:04:24,331 --> 00:04:26,742
Right now.

28
00:04:26,766 --> 00:04:28,744
I don't even know where she is.

29
00:04:28,768 --> 00:04:32,415
Try Michelle Thomas.

30
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:34,150
I don't know the number.

31
00:04:34,174 --> 00:04:36,455
It's a new listing.

32
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:43,426
<i>No. I said goodbye,</i>
and I meant goodbye.

33
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:45,895
Andrea, you just can't
walk out on me like this.

34
00:04:45,919 --> 00:04:47,664
I just did.

35
00:04:47,688 --> 00:04:48,765
I love you.

36
00:04:48,789 --> 00:04:50,355
I had too much to drink.

37
00:04:52,192 --> 00:04:54,304
I'll change. I promise.

38
00:04:54,328 --> 00:04:56,372
I wish I could believe you.

39
00:04:56,396 --> 00:04:57,707
Come on over tonight.

40
00:04:57,731 --> 00:05:00,610
No, I can't. I can't.

41
00:05:00,634 --> 00:05:02,445
Look, sweetheart,
you left a lot of things.

42
00:05:02,469 --> 00:05:04,909
So just come by and
get what you need.

43
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:07,698
At least give me a chance
to apologize, all right?

44
00:05:09,476 --> 00:05:10,508
Okay.

45
00:05:12,279 --> 00:05:15,325
Great. Hey, I love you.

46
00:05:20,554 --> 00:05:21,965
Don't do it. Don't go.

47
00:05:21,989 --> 00:05:24,734
I'm just going to pick
up the rest of my things.

48
00:05:24,758 --> 00:05:26,736
Maybe this will help
to get him off my back

49
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,638
and out of my mind
once and for all.

50
00:05:28,662 --> 00:05:31,274
Or it might provoke
him. I'll go with you.

51
00:05:31,298 --> 00:05:33,476
No, no, Michelle.

52
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:37,313
He's hit me for the last time.

53
00:05:37,337 --> 00:05:41,217
I won't let that happen
again, no matter what it takes.

54
00:06:57,117 --> 00:06:58,984
What the...?

55
00:07:02,255 --> 00:07:03,232
Oh.

56
00:07:03,256 --> 00:07:05,356
Ow. Ow.

57
00:07:10,196 --> 00:07:11,707
Clay?

58
00:07:11,731 --> 00:07:14,091
Arthur?

59
00:07:14,968 --> 00:07:16,879
I'm down here.

60
00:07:16,903 --> 00:07:19,449
Well, the
electricity's gone out.

61
00:07:19,473 --> 00:07:20,649
I wouldn't know.

62
00:07:20,673 --> 00:07:22,284
Yeah, it's... It's
a circuit breaker.

63
00:07:22,308 --> 00:07:23,753
What are you doing here, anyway?

64
00:07:23,777 --> 00:07:25,855
Just taking care of
some old business.

65
00:07:25,879 --> 00:07:28,824
Arthur? Yeah, it's me, Clay.

66
00:07:28,848 --> 00:07:30,726
All alone in the dark.

67
00:07:33,186 --> 00:07:34,186
Oh!

68
00:08:03,951 --> 00:08:05,917
Clay?

69
00:08:08,688 --> 00:08:10,588
Clay.

70
00:08:13,427 --> 00:08:15,526
Clay.

71
00:08:17,197 --> 00:08:19,708
Oh. Clay.

72
00:08:22,069 --> 00:08:23,446
<i>Security patrol.</i>

73
00:08:23,470 --> 00:08:26,938
Uh, hello. Uh, I'd like
to report a gunshot.

74
00:08:47,761 --> 00:08:49,138
Hello?

75
00:08:49,162 --> 00:08:50,439
Ar... Arthur?

76
00:08:50,463 --> 00:08:54,209
Andrea. <i>Arthur, I...</i>
Is something wrong?

77
00:08:54,233 --> 00:08:56,045
Oh, God.

78
00:08:56,069 --> 00:08:57,447
Arthur, Clay's
dead, and there's...

79
00:08:57,471 --> 00:08:58,915
I don't know what happened.

80
00:08:58,939 --> 00:09:01,084
<i>There's blood. He's been
shot.</i> Andrea, calm down.

81
00:09:01,108 --> 00:09:02,552
<i>Arthur, I'm scared.</i>
Now, calm down.

82
00:09:02,576 --> 00:09:04,487
What should I do?
Should I call the police?

83
00:09:04,511 --> 00:09:06,422
No. Listen to me. <i>But Arthur...</i>

84
00:09:06,446 --> 00:09:08,524
Andrea, listen to me.

85
00:09:08,548 --> 00:09:11,393
I want you to leave the
house and come over here.

86
00:09:11,417 --> 00:09:14,797
I'll call a lawyer, and
then we'll call the police.

87
00:09:14,821 --> 00:09:16,532
<i>Have you got that?</i>

88
00:09:16,556 --> 00:09:18,367
Okay. Okay.

89
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:20,124
Now, get out of there.

90
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:02,111
Good morning, Cassie.

91
00:10:02,135 --> 00:10:03,479
Good morning.

92
00:10:03,503 --> 00:10:05,581
How's your cat? Cat's fine.

93
00:10:05,605 --> 00:10:07,984
How's your rabbit?
Rabbit's fine.

94
00:10:08,008 --> 00:10:11,609
Your philodendrons?
Philodendrons' fine.

95
00:10:12,779 --> 00:10:14,223
What's that?

96
00:10:14,247 --> 00:10:16,759
It's a present. Hm.

97
00:10:16,783 --> 00:10:18,761
Open it.

98
00:10:18,785 --> 00:10:20,096
Who's it from?

99
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:23,199
I think it's from Michelle. Huh.

100
00:10:23,223 --> 00:10:24,989
Open it.

101
00:10:35,502 --> 00:10:37,112
Hm.

102
00:10:37,136 --> 00:10:38,414
It's a tie.

103
00:10:38,438 --> 00:10:39,737
Yeah.

104
00:10:41,774 --> 00:10:43,786
I don't like it.

105
00:10:43,810 --> 00:10:45,755
You're gonna tell
Michelle Thomas,

106
00:10:45,779 --> 00:10:48,090
who moved here all the
way from London, England,

107
00:10:48,114 --> 00:10:52,028
that you don't like the tie
she bought for you out of love?

108
00:10:52,052 --> 00:10:53,362
Well, i-it's just...

109
00:10:53,386 --> 00:10:56,365
It's a gift from a person.

110
00:10:56,389 --> 00:10:59,268
You should've seen how excited
she was when she left it here.

111
00:10:59,292 --> 00:11:01,104
And it's very expensive,

112
00:11:01,128 --> 00:11:03,694
And you're going to tell
her that you don't like it?

113
00:11:04,765 --> 00:11:07,110
No.

114
00:11:07,134 --> 00:11:08,900
No, I like it.

115
00:11:10,203 --> 00:11:12,014
Where is she so I can thank her?

116
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:13,750
Oh, she's down at the jail.

117
00:11:13,774 --> 00:11:15,284
Girlfriend of hers
was just arrested

118
00:11:15,308 --> 00:11:18,253
for murdering Clay
Graham. You hear about that?

119
00:11:18,277 --> 00:11:19,555
No.

120
00:11:19,579 --> 00:11:22,157
Oh, I guess he was
sort of a man about town.

121
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:24,114
Andrea Morrow is her name.

122
00:11:25,519 --> 00:11:29,365
Is, uh, Michelle thinking
about representing her?

123
00:11:29,389 --> 00:11:31,768
Well, I... I don't think she
really has much choice.

124
00:11:31,792 --> 00:11:33,669
I mean, they've been
friends for a long time,

125
00:11:33,693 --> 00:11:35,471
and it doesn't really
look very good for her.

126
00:11:35,495 --> 00:11:37,240
Andrea, I mean.

127
00:11:37,264 --> 00:11:39,141
Oh.

128
00:11:39,165 --> 00:11:42,600
Oh. That tie
really looks terrific.

129
00:11:44,203 --> 00:11:46,582
You don't think it's too bright?

130
00:11:46,606 --> 00:11:49,118
No. No, I-I think
it makes you look

131
00:11:49,142 --> 00:11:51,120
like a real class act.

132
00:11:51,144 --> 00:11:53,344
It sort of sets off your hair.

133
00:11:57,216 --> 00:11:59,628
Down at the jail, huh? Mm-hm.

134
00:11:59,652 --> 00:12:03,165
Someone grabbed me from behind
and knocked me out somehow.

135
00:12:03,189 --> 00:12:05,334
And then when I came
to, Clay was dead,

136
00:12:05,358 --> 00:12:06,635
and his gun was in my purse.

137
00:12:06,659 --> 00:12:08,571
I-I don't know anything else.

138
00:12:08,595 --> 00:12:10,272
I just wish whoever
had knocked you out

139
00:12:10,296 --> 00:12:13,142
had left a nice bump on
your head, or used chloroform.

140
00:12:13,166 --> 00:12:15,606
Anything that would
substantiate your story.

141
00:12:17,170 --> 00:12:18,747
Good morning. Oh. Hi.

142
00:12:18,771 --> 00:12:20,983
Andrea Morrow,
this is Ben Matlock.

143
00:12:21,007 --> 00:12:23,485
Uh, I was just in
the neighborhood.

144
00:12:23,509 --> 00:12:25,487
Ben, could I speak to
you outside for a second?

145
00:12:25,511 --> 00:12:28,279
Uh, sure. Uh, excuse us.

146
00:12:36,056 --> 00:12:38,234
Ben, she was in
Clay Graham's home

147
00:12:38,258 --> 00:12:39,702
at the time the
murder took place.

148
00:12:39,726 --> 00:12:42,171
Her fingerprints were
found on the murder weapon.

149
00:12:42,195 --> 00:12:44,340
She appeared to be
fleeing the scene of the crime

150
00:12:44,364 --> 00:12:46,175
when she was apprehended.

151
00:12:46,199 --> 00:12:48,677
And she made some damaging
statements to the police

152
00:12:48,701 --> 00:12:50,446
about how Graham
abused her physically.

153
00:12:50,470 --> 00:12:51,769
Hm.

154
00:12:52,839 --> 00:12:55,217
Hm.

155
00:12:55,241 --> 00:12:56,919
Do you think she did it?

156
00:12:56,943 --> 00:12:58,209
Not for an instant.

157
00:12:59,345 --> 00:13:01,312
Well, that's a start.

158
00:13:02,682 --> 00:13:04,160
Ben, she's an old friend.

159
00:13:04,184 --> 00:13:05,628
She doesn't have a lot of money.

160
00:13:05,652 --> 00:13:07,596
Yeah, I heard.

161
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:09,632
I don't think I'm
ready to take on

162
00:13:09,656 --> 00:13:12,034
a case like this entirely alone.

163
00:13:12,058 --> 00:13:13,469
I mean, what if I should lose?

164
00:13:13,493 --> 00:13:15,192
What an awful prospect.

165
00:13:16,997 --> 00:13:19,041
Oh.

166
00:13:19,065 --> 00:13:24,602
Well... maybe... together.

167
00:13:25,672 --> 00:13:27,016
Oh, thank you.

168
00:13:31,277 --> 00:13:32,754
Thanks for my tie.

169
00:13:32,778 --> 00:13:34,458
You like it? I love it.

170
00:13:35,781 --> 00:13:38,221
If I'd known this was coming
up, I'd have bought you a suit.

171
00:13:39,319 --> 00:13:41,764
Well, let... Let's give
the case our best shot,

172
00:13:41,788 --> 00:13:45,155
and take a long
time about the suit.

173
00:14:14,754 --> 00:14:17,566
Well, how'd the bail hearing go?

174
00:14:17,590 --> 00:14:19,735
Fine.

175
00:14:19,759 --> 00:14:21,458
What's the matter?

176
00:14:22,762 --> 00:14:24,139
Andrea said something to me

177
00:14:24,163 --> 00:14:25,741
before she went to
see Clay Graham.

178
00:14:25,765 --> 00:14:27,643
What?

179
00:14:27,667 --> 00:14:30,546
She said she'd never
let him hit her again.

180
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:32,014
I can't fault her for that.

181
00:14:32,038 --> 00:14:34,398
"No matter what it takes?"

182
00:14:35,775 --> 00:14:37,553
The DA knows she was with me

183
00:14:37,577 --> 00:14:40,222
just before she
went to see Graham.

184
00:14:40,246 --> 00:14:43,213
What if they call me to
testify to her state of mind?

185
00:14:44,483 --> 00:14:46,461
Well, you could...
You could claim

186
00:14:46,485 --> 00:14:48,296
lawyer-client privilege.

187
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:50,132
Yeah, but I wasn't
her lawyer then.

188
00:14:50,156 --> 00:14:51,466
Well, the DA doesn't know that,

189
00:14:51,490 --> 00:14:53,235
and you don't have to tell him.

190
00:14:53,259 --> 00:14:54,637
Well, it's a problem.

191
00:14:54,661 --> 00:14:58,040
Well, maybe it
won't turn out to be.

192
00:14:58,064 --> 00:15:00,709
You have to play
those things by ear.

193
00:15:00,733 --> 00:15:02,411
That's odd.

194
00:15:02,435 --> 00:15:04,080
What?

195
00:15:04,104 --> 00:15:06,048
He's left his key in the car.

196
00:15:07,540 --> 00:15:09,385
Uh, digital lock
and alarm system.

197
00:15:09,409 --> 00:15:10,719
You gotta punch in a code.

198
00:15:10,743 --> 00:15:12,621
Yeah.

199
00:15:12,645 --> 00:15:15,224
If Clay Graham left
his keys in the car,

200
00:15:15,248 --> 00:15:17,459
how'd he get in the house?

201
00:15:19,452 --> 00:15:22,031
Hm. Watch your foot.

202
00:15:22,055 --> 00:15:24,099
Ooh.

203
00:15:24,123 --> 00:15:26,401
Well, that explains the cut
on the bottom of his right foot.

204
00:15:26,425 --> 00:15:28,092
Oh, yeah.

205
00:15:30,730 --> 00:15:32,474
What time you got?

206
00:15:32,498 --> 00:15:35,443
Ten forty-five. Uh, me too.

207
00:15:35,467 --> 00:15:37,880
Clock's off. Five minutes slow.

208
00:15:37,904 --> 00:15:39,782
So's the one in here.

209
00:15:39,806 --> 00:15:41,317
Five minutes slow? Strange.

210
00:15:41,341 --> 00:15:43,307
Look at that. Power outage.

211
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:45,921
Wonder if he was
taping something

212
00:15:45,945 --> 00:15:47,756
the night he was murdered?

213
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:49,424
Why don't you give it a listen?

214
00:15:49,448 --> 00:15:51,059
I'll see if there
was a power outage

215
00:15:51,083 --> 00:15:52,363
in the whole neighborhood.

216
00:16:14,807 --> 00:16:16,218
Mr. Hampton? Mr. Matlock?

217
00:16:16,242 --> 00:16:18,354
Yes. I-I'm sorry.

218
00:16:18,378 --> 00:16:20,155
I didn't... I didn't
mean to startle you.

219
00:16:20,179 --> 00:16:21,723
You didn't. Please come in.

220
00:16:21,747 --> 00:16:24,126
Make yourself comfortable.

221
00:16:27,052 --> 00:16:29,664
Well... Andrea said

222
00:16:29,688 --> 00:16:31,666
that you were remarkable,

223
00:16:31,690 --> 00:16:34,210
but I never expected this.

224
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:36,871
You do all this
sculpture? Yes, sir.

225
00:16:38,764 --> 00:16:41,343
'Course I'm not an
expert on art or anything,

226
00:16:41,367 --> 00:16:43,245
but it seems to me

227
00:16:43,269 --> 00:16:46,237
it's downright beautiful.

228
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:48,440
Well, thank you.

229
00:16:50,776 --> 00:16:53,096
Clay Graham was a good friend.

230
00:16:54,614 --> 00:16:57,025
Yes. Yes, he was.

231
00:16:57,049 --> 00:16:58,182
He was very helpful.

232
00:16:59,519 --> 00:17:01,029
He also had a lot of money,

233
00:17:01,053 --> 00:17:04,100
and most of the
people he knew did too.

234
00:17:04,124 --> 00:17:08,704
Uh, he... also had
a guilty conscience.

235
00:17:08,728 --> 00:17:10,106
I... I don't know.

236
00:17:10,130 --> 00:17:11,974
I assume Andrea
told you the full story.

237
00:17:11,998 --> 00:17:15,244
Well, she said that, uh,

238
00:17:15,268 --> 00:17:18,380
you and Clay had met
about eight years ago

239
00:17:18,404 --> 00:17:20,382
through mutual acquaintances,

240
00:17:20,406 --> 00:17:23,586
and then after you
had become friends

241
00:17:23,610 --> 00:17:24,720
about seven years ago,

242
00:17:24,744 --> 00:17:28,123
you were both in an
automobile accident.

243
00:17:28,147 --> 00:17:30,626
That's right. We
were at a party.

244
00:17:30,650 --> 00:17:33,362
Had been some
drinking, of course.

245
00:17:33,386 --> 00:17:36,732
I-I thought Clay was all right,

246
00:17:36,756 --> 00:17:38,334
but he lost control of the car.

247
00:17:38,358 --> 00:17:41,237
He skidded off
the road, hit a tree.

248
00:17:41,261 --> 00:17:43,739
He got away with
minor scratches.

249
00:17:43,763 --> 00:17:47,576
I... wasn't so lucky.

250
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:50,146
Hm. And you...
You became an artist

251
00:17:50,170 --> 00:17:54,150
suddenly who had
to... work from memory.

252
00:17:54,174 --> 00:17:56,619
Well, that's not...

253
00:17:56,643 --> 00:17:58,320
entirely accurate, Mr. Matlock.

254
00:17:58,344 --> 00:18:00,578
If you'll step over here,
I'll... I'll demonstrate.

255
00:18:06,452 --> 00:18:09,698
People still pose for me.

256
00:18:09,722 --> 00:18:10,855
Hm.

257
00:18:13,359 --> 00:18:15,704
At first, I hated doing this.

258
00:18:15,728 --> 00:18:17,806
Uh, and then I
realized this gives me

259
00:18:17,830 --> 00:18:19,196
quite an advantage.

260
00:18:20,699 --> 00:18:22,711
People have no idea how
much insight I can gain

261
00:18:22,735 --> 00:18:24,379
into their character
from doing this.

262
00:18:24,403 --> 00:18:25,714
Hm.

263
00:18:25,738 --> 00:18:27,271
Hm. Hm.

264
00:18:29,242 --> 00:18:31,052
Hm.

265
00:18:31,076 --> 00:18:32,309
Hm.

266
00:18:33,513 --> 00:18:35,353
Um... Hm.

267
00:18:36,282 --> 00:18:39,494
I wanted to ask you about
that phone call you made

268
00:18:39,518 --> 00:18:43,098
to Clay Graham's house
the night of the murder.

269
00:18:43,122 --> 00:18:44,266
Yes.

270
00:18:44,290 --> 00:18:46,257
W-what was it about?

271
00:18:48,261 --> 00:18:51,507
Well, I... I knew
that he'd talked

272
00:18:51,531 --> 00:18:54,042
Andrea into coming over,

273
00:18:54,066 --> 00:18:56,178
and I was worried about
what would happen.

274
00:18:56,202 --> 00:18:59,515
With good reason,
as it turned out.

275
00:18:59,539 --> 00:19:00,816
Do you think she did it?

276
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:03,352
What other explanation is there?

277
00:19:03,376 --> 00:19:05,988
I don't know.

278
00:19:06,012 --> 00:19:07,211
I don't know.

279
00:19:08,581 --> 00:19:11,916
Uh, why did you advise her to
leave the scene of the crime?

280
00:19:13,119 --> 00:19:14,696
That was a mistake.

281
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:16,899
I know that now.

282
00:19:16,923 --> 00:19:19,568
But she was hysterical.

283
00:19:19,592 --> 00:19:21,970
If she'd talked to the
police in that condition,

284
00:19:21,994 --> 00:19:25,540
uh, God alone knows what
would've come out of her mouth.

285
00:19:25,564 --> 00:19:28,710
I just thought she
should calm down first.

286
00:19:28,734 --> 00:19:30,678
By the way, I'd be happy
to say that on the stand,

287
00:19:30,702 --> 00:19:31,713
if that would help.

288
00:19:31,737 --> 00:19:33,716
Oh, well, thank you.

289
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:35,940
I don't know that
that'll be necessary.

290
00:19:36,743 --> 00:19:39,721
Uh, I... I'll be going. And
thank you for your time.

291
00:19:39,745 --> 00:19:42,591
Of course. Okay.

292
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:52,422
♪ Well, you get a line
I'll get a pole, honey ♪

293
00:19:53,793 --> 00:19:57,372
♪ You get a line And
I'll get a pole, babe ♪

294
00:19:57,396 --> 00:19:59,341
♪ You get a line
And I'll get a pole ♪

295
00:19:59,365 --> 00:20:01,443
♪ We'll row down
To the crawdad hole ♪

296
00:20:01,467 --> 00:20:04,713
♪ Honey, baby, mine ♪

297
00:20:04,737 --> 00:20:07,904
♪ Well, get up, Kate
You slept too late, honey ♪

298
00:20:09,107 --> 00:20:11,753
♪ Get up, Kate You
slept too late, babe ♪

299
00:20:11,777 --> 00:20:13,655
Oh, oh.

300
00:20:13,679 --> 00:20:15,290
You know what my
mother used to say.

301
00:20:15,314 --> 00:20:17,326
Them's good eats.

302
00:20:17,350 --> 00:20:18,350
Mm.

303
00:20:19,352 --> 00:20:20,495
When you have a cold like that,

304
00:20:20,519 --> 00:20:22,163
aren't you supposed
to starve it?

305
00:20:22,187 --> 00:20:23,253
Mm-mm.

306
00:20:25,090 --> 00:20:26,668
Feed it. MICHELLE: Hi.

307
00:20:26,692 --> 00:20:28,571
No, thanks.

308
00:20:28,595 --> 00:20:29,772
I went to the radio station.

309
00:20:29,796 --> 00:20:31,774
The one that Clay Graham
was taping off the air.

310
00:20:31,798 --> 00:20:34,118
There's a five-minute
gap in the tape.

311
00:20:35,267 --> 00:20:36,979
Could you establish the time?

312
00:20:37,003 --> 00:20:38,780
Well, I played it for
them. The music cuts off

313
00:20:38,804 --> 00:20:41,249
at what had to
have been 9:59 p.m.,

314
00:20:41,273 --> 00:20:43,084
and then comes back
on again at the end

315
00:20:43,108 --> 00:20:45,587
of the hourly
newsbreak at 10:04 p.m.

316
00:20:45,611 --> 00:20:48,056
Mm.

317
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:49,824
Okay.

318
00:20:49,848 --> 00:20:51,526
Okay.

319
00:20:51,550 --> 00:20:52,710
There you are.

320
00:20:54,586 --> 00:20:55,697
Yeah.

321
00:20:55,721 --> 00:20:58,199
Highway security
received that night call

322
00:20:58,223 --> 00:21:00,602
about the gunshot at 10:10

323
00:21:00,626 --> 00:21:03,438
and arrived on
the scene at 10:19.

324
00:21:03,462 --> 00:21:06,742
Did Andrea say anything
about the, uh, power being off?

325
00:21:06,766 --> 00:21:09,444
No. Maybe it was turned off

326
00:21:09,468 --> 00:21:12,213
after she was knocked
out, and back on

327
00:21:12,237 --> 00:21:13,749
before she came to.

328
00:21:13,773 --> 00:21:16,117
The lights were off,
what, five minutes?

329
00:21:16,141 --> 00:21:17,686
Yeah.

330
00:21:17,710 --> 00:21:20,456
Are you saying that the
killer put out the lights,

331
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:22,758
and then he shot Clay
Graham from 20 feet away

332
00:21:22,782 --> 00:21:24,493
in total darkness?

333
00:21:24,517 --> 00:21:27,028
It's sure starting to
be a real possibility.

334
00:21:27,052 --> 00:21:28,363
Ladies.

335
00:21:28,387 --> 00:21:31,066
I understand you have the
need of a private investigator.

336
00:21:31,090 --> 00:21:32,767
That's right. Cassie, uh,

337
00:21:32,791 --> 00:21:35,470
run a background on...
On Arthur Hampton.

338
00:21:35,494 --> 00:21:38,540
You know, property, voter
registration, all that stuff.

339
00:21:38,564 --> 00:21:41,109
And Tyler, this... This
fellow Hampton's an artist.

340
00:21:41,133 --> 00:21:43,545
His studio's downtown. Okay.

341
00:21:43,569 --> 00:21:47,048
See if he was a Green
Beret or into martial arts.

342
00:21:47,072 --> 00:21:49,150
An artist into the martial arts.

343
00:21:49,174 --> 00:21:52,153
We need to know if he
could knock somebody out

344
00:21:52,177 --> 00:21:53,455
without leaving a bruise.

345
00:21:53,479 --> 00:21:55,523
No bruise.

346
00:21:55,547 --> 00:21:56,758
He's blind.

347
00:21:56,782 --> 00:22:00,028
A blind artist into
the martial arts.

348
00:22:00,052 --> 00:22:03,398
Check his alibi for the night
Clay Graham was murdered.

349
00:22:03,422 --> 00:22:04,699
Check his alibi.

350
00:22:04,723 --> 00:22:07,136
Ben, you just said
the man was blind.

351
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:09,037
How much more of
an alibi do you need?

352
00:22:10,863 --> 00:22:11,974
Hey.

353
00:22:11,998 --> 00:22:14,977
A blind artist into
martial arts. No.

354
00:22:15,001 --> 00:22:16,178
No bruise.

355
00:22:43,796 --> 00:22:44,995
Mr. Hampton?

356
00:22:47,466 --> 00:22:49,545
Mr. Matlock.

357
00:22:49,569 --> 00:22:52,180
Your answering service
said you were down here.

358
00:22:52,204 --> 00:22:55,684
I... I couldn't imagine why.

359
00:22:55,708 --> 00:22:58,386
I've been subpoenaed
by the prosecution.

360
00:22:58,410 --> 00:23:01,378
I came down here to... rehearse.

361
00:23:02,714 --> 00:23:04,693
Oh.

362
00:23:04,717 --> 00:23:06,428
That's very impressive.

363
00:23:06,452 --> 00:23:09,631
Well, the idea of being led to

364
00:23:09,655 --> 00:23:12,668
or stumbling my way
to the witness box...

365
00:23:12,692 --> 00:23:14,236
You understand?

366
00:23:14,260 --> 00:23:21,064
Um, well, I... I
s... I suppose I do.

367
00:23:22,335 --> 00:23:24,346
You wanna see me?

368
00:23:24,370 --> 00:23:25,747
Well, yes.

369
00:23:25,771 --> 00:23:29,150
Um... Uh, this is a...

370
00:23:29,174 --> 00:23:31,653
This is a little awkward.

371
00:23:31,677 --> 00:23:34,478
I'll try not to notice.

372
00:23:44,690 --> 00:23:47,301
Well...

373
00:23:47,325 --> 00:23:49,404
where were you at
the time of the murder?

374
00:23:51,597 --> 00:23:54,376
I was at home all
night, Mr. Matlock.

375
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:57,045
Alone. Reading.

376
00:23:57,069 --> 00:23:58,246
Braille.

377
00:23:58,270 --> 00:23:59,714
I see.

378
00:23:59,738 --> 00:24:05,008
You don't... seriously
suspect me, do you?

379
00:24:07,046 --> 00:24:08,957
Well, I have reason to believe

380
00:24:08,981 --> 00:24:11,193
that the electricity
was turned off

381
00:24:11,217 --> 00:24:13,061
in Clay Graham's home

382
00:24:13,085 --> 00:24:14,751
just before he was murdered.

383
00:24:16,321 --> 00:24:19,701
Thus giving the killer
the element of surprise.

384
00:24:19,725 --> 00:24:22,693
Or evening up the odds.

385
00:24:24,096 --> 00:24:26,230
Mr. Matlock...

386
00:24:33,772 --> 00:24:36,706
You should be ashamed
for even thinking such a thing.

387
00:24:38,544 --> 00:24:40,784
Clay Graham was my best friend.

388
00:24:41,481 --> 00:24:44,915
I wouldn't have killed
him even if I could. Ah.

389
00:24:47,753 --> 00:24:52,089
Quite obviously... I can't.

390
00:24:55,761 --> 00:24:59,408
I understand that you
were quite a marksman

391
00:24:59,432 --> 00:25:01,231
before you lost your sight.

392
00:25:02,568 --> 00:25:07,437
Mr. Matlock, I ask
you... truthfully...

393
00:25:11,176 --> 00:25:13,076
are these the eyes of a killer?

394
00:25:25,457 --> 00:25:28,091
Don't forget to
turn out the lights.

395
00:25:49,782 --> 00:25:52,616
Uh. Cassie.

396
00:25:55,688 --> 00:25:58,266
Well.

397
00:25:58,290 --> 00:25:59,601
What's this now?

398
00:25:59,625 --> 00:26:01,169
It's a present.

399
00:26:01,193 --> 00:26:02,671
Oh. Open it.

400
00:26:05,664 --> 00:26:08,310
What is it? Open it.

401
00:26:18,644 --> 00:26:20,722
Oh, it's... It's a shirt.

402
00:26:20,746 --> 00:26:22,746
It's beautiful. Mm.

403
00:26:25,084 --> 00:26:27,662
Hm. It's pink.

404
00:26:27,686 --> 00:26:29,263
Oh, it's okay for
men to wear pink.

405
00:26:29,287 --> 00:26:30,732
It'll look great on you.

406
00:26:30,756 --> 00:26:33,234
And it'll also go with that
tie that Michelle bought you.

407
00:26:33,258 --> 00:26:36,470
And suspenders are
really the rage right now.

408
00:26:36,494 --> 00:26:37,638
Who's it from?

409
00:26:37,662 --> 00:26:38,974
From both of us.

410
00:26:38,998 --> 00:26:40,575
Out of love.

411
00:26:40,599 --> 00:26:42,311
Oh.

412
00:26:42,335 --> 00:26:44,046
Is one of you Michelle Thomas?

413
00:26:44,070 --> 00:26:45,314
Not me.

414
00:26:45,338 --> 00:26:47,482
I'm Michelle Thomas.

415
00:26:47,506 --> 00:26:49,684
This is from the
district attorney's office.

416
00:26:49,708 --> 00:26:51,386
Subpoena.

417
00:26:51,410 --> 00:26:52,776
Have a good day.

418
00:26:55,614 --> 00:26:58,126
Well, I guess the DA paid
attention to Andrea's statement.

419
00:26:59,185 --> 00:27:01,629
Well, he's just doing his job.

420
00:27:01,653 --> 00:27:03,653
Oh, boy. I really dread this.

421
00:27:10,029 --> 00:27:13,575
State calls Michelle Thomas.

422
00:27:13,599 --> 00:27:15,710
Uh, Miss Thomas...

423
00:27:15,734 --> 00:27:17,345
the defendant moved in with you

424
00:27:17,369 --> 00:27:20,048
because she just broke
up with Clay Graham.

425
00:27:20,072 --> 00:27:22,183
Is that correct? Yes.

426
00:27:22,207 --> 00:27:25,520
And you spoke with her
on the evening of the murder

427
00:27:25,544 --> 00:27:27,255
before she went over
to Mr. Graham's house.

428
00:27:27,279 --> 00:27:28,490
Is that correct?

429
00:27:28,514 --> 00:27:30,726
Yes. She was going to
get some of her things.

430
00:27:30,750 --> 00:27:33,127
You knew at that
time that Mr. Graham

431
00:27:33,151 --> 00:27:35,497
had a history of violence
towards the defendant?

432
00:27:35,521 --> 00:27:36,898
Yes.

433
00:27:36,922 --> 00:27:38,499
When he'd gotten angry,

434
00:27:38,523 --> 00:27:39,734
he had been known to strike her.

435
00:27:39,758 --> 00:27:41,737
Is that correct? Yes.

436
00:27:41,761 --> 00:27:45,807
And it was Miss Morrow's idea to
move out of Mr. Graham's house?

437
00:27:45,831 --> 00:27:47,876
Yes. That's what I understood.

438
00:27:47,900 --> 00:27:50,745
And Mr. Graham wasn't too
happy about that, was he?

439
00:27:50,769 --> 00:27:52,080
No.

440
00:27:52,104 --> 00:27:54,115
Miss Thomas, did you
discuss with the defendant

441
00:27:54,139 --> 00:27:57,219
the fact that she
might face some danger

442
00:27:57,243 --> 00:28:01,156
in going over to
Mr. Graham's house that night?

443
00:28:01,180 --> 00:28:02,260
Yes.

444
00:28:03,481 --> 00:28:04,647
What did she say?

445
00:28:06,885 --> 00:28:09,764
She said she was
nervous about going.

446
00:28:09,788 --> 00:28:11,700
Is that all?

447
00:28:11,724 --> 00:28:13,134
Uh, you're her friend.

448
00:28:13,158 --> 00:28:15,670
You didn't tell her not to go,

449
00:28:15,694 --> 00:28:18,472
or maybe perhaps
go along with her?

450
00:28:18,496 --> 00:28:20,575
Well, sure, I did.

451
00:28:20,599 --> 00:28:25,514
What did the defendant
say, Miss Thomas?

452
00:28:25,538 --> 00:28:27,715
I don't recall her exact words.

453
00:28:27,739 --> 00:28:30,574
Well, then, would you
paraphrase it for us, please?

454
00:28:32,744 --> 00:28:36,724
She said no... uh,

455
00:28:36,748 --> 00:28:38,059
he'd hit her for the last time,

456
00:28:38,083 --> 00:28:39,694
and she wasn't gonna
let him do it again.

457
00:28:39,718 --> 00:28:42,198
No matter what it takes.

458
00:28:42,754 --> 00:28:46,156
Thank you. No further
questions, Your Honor.

459
00:28:48,493 --> 00:28:50,072
Cross, Mr. Matlock?

460
00:28:50,096 --> 00:28:53,275
I'd like to examine the phrase,

461
00:28:53,299 --> 00:28:55,176
"No matter what it takes."

462
00:28:55,200 --> 00:28:59,046
Seems to me that's a phrase that
most of us use almost every day.

463
00:28:59,070 --> 00:29:01,216
I'll do such and such,
"no matter what it takes."

464
00:29:01,240 --> 00:29:04,252
When I was 11 or 12 years old,

465
00:29:04,276 --> 00:29:06,654
my mother said, "Next
Sunday, you're gonna learn

466
00:29:06,678 --> 00:29:08,423
to make cornbread,
no matter what it takes."

467
00:29:08,447 --> 00:29:09,891
And I don't think
she meant to kill me.

468
00:29:09,915 --> 00:29:11,659
Objection, Your Honor.

469
00:29:11,683 --> 00:29:13,094
Nothing further.

470
00:29:13,118 --> 00:29:14,951
Objection, Your Honor.

471
00:29:17,089 --> 00:29:20,502
Uh, the state calls
Arthur Hampton

472
00:29:20,526 --> 00:29:22,359
to the stand.

473
00:29:24,329 --> 00:29:25,606
Let me give you a hand there.

474
00:29:25,630 --> 00:29:27,350
Thank you. That
won't be necessary.

475
00:29:46,618 --> 00:29:48,730
What was the gist
of the conversation

476
00:29:48,754 --> 00:29:51,966
that Mr. Graham had with
Miss Morrow that afternoon,

477
00:29:51,990 --> 00:29:53,367
when he called her
from your studio?

478
00:29:53,391 --> 00:29:54,636
Do you recall that?

479
00:29:54,660 --> 00:29:56,505
Yes. He asked her
to come by that night

480
00:29:56,529 --> 00:29:58,673
and collect the
rest of her things.

481
00:29:58,697 --> 00:30:01,376
And that they were going
to be there alone, correct?

482
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:04,067
Yes. Well, that
was the whole idea.

483
00:30:05,770 --> 00:30:09,350
I know that because...

484
00:30:09,374 --> 00:30:13,288
I'm afraid the idea
was mostly mine.

485
00:30:13,312 --> 00:30:16,757
See, I knew that Clay cared
a lot more about Andrea

486
00:30:16,781 --> 00:30:19,727
than he was willing to say.

487
00:30:19,751 --> 00:30:21,162
And as a friend,

488
00:30:21,186 --> 00:30:24,454
I urged him to try
to reconcile with her.

489
00:30:25,724 --> 00:30:28,736
That is advice
that I will always

490
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:31,061
regret giving.

491
00:30:32,431 --> 00:30:35,331
Andrea, I'm sorry.

492
00:30:37,802 --> 00:30:39,780
I should've known that
Clay would force you

493
00:30:39,804 --> 00:30:40,781
to do what you did.

494
00:30:40,805 --> 00:30:42,016
Objection.

495
00:30:42,040 --> 00:30:43,784
Move to strike that statement.

496
00:30:43,808 --> 00:30:45,786
Assumes facts not in evidence.

497
00:30:45,810 --> 00:30:47,722
Request the jury be admonished.

498
00:30:47,746 --> 00:30:49,724
Sustained.

499
00:30:49,748 --> 00:30:53,028
You will completely disregard
the witness's last remark.

500
00:30:53,052 --> 00:30:54,918
I have no further
questions, Your Honor.

501
00:30:56,455 --> 00:30:57,765
Cross, Mr. Matlock?

502
00:30:57,789 --> 00:30:58,799
Uh, no, Your Honor.

503
00:30:58,823 --> 00:31:00,168
But the defense
reserves the right

504
00:31:00,192 --> 00:31:02,804
to recall this witness
at a future time.

505
00:31:06,765 --> 00:31:08,576
He was very subtle, Mr. Hampton.

506
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:10,500
Subtle? He buried us.

507
00:31:11,636 --> 00:31:12,936
You got a point.

508
00:32:03,188 --> 00:32:05,228
Thank you, guys.
It was a terrific job.

509
00:34:15,621 --> 00:34:17,220
Lovely perfume, Miss Thomas.

510
00:34:18,557 --> 00:34:22,503
I noticed it the other
day in the courtroom.

511
00:34:22,527 --> 00:34:24,861
And I noticed it here
earlier this evening.

512
00:34:29,401 --> 00:34:31,601
Where are you, Michelle?

513
00:34:40,411 --> 00:34:41,911
At least let me explain.

514
00:34:50,622 --> 00:34:53,468
I have a weapon...

515
00:34:53,492 --> 00:34:57,226
because I often work
here... late at night.

516
00:34:59,063 --> 00:35:01,364
I have it for protection.

517
00:35:04,136 --> 00:35:07,570
Never know when you're
gonna be surprised by an intruder.

518
00:35:12,677 --> 00:35:13,677
Bang.

519
00:35:16,748 --> 00:35:18,081
You're dead.

520
00:35:35,534 --> 00:35:38,746
Andrea said Hampton came
and went here as he pleased.

521
00:35:38,770 --> 00:35:41,148
So he had all the time in the
world to set up the shooting

522
00:35:41,172 --> 00:35:43,250
the way he did in
his studio. Mm-hm.

523
00:35:43,274 --> 00:35:44,385
He must've, um...

524
00:35:44,409 --> 00:35:47,388
Must've set up a
string somewhere.

525
00:35:47,412 --> 00:35:50,491
Somewhere along here. So he
knew the exact angle to shoot at

526
00:35:50,515 --> 00:35:52,393
from the bottom of the stairs.

527
00:35:52,417 --> 00:35:54,228
And then he went
back to his studio

528
00:35:54,252 --> 00:35:55,463
and did some target practice.

529
00:35:55,487 --> 00:35:57,197
Mm.

530
00:35:57,221 --> 00:35:58,788
Are you paying attention?

531
00:36:00,625 --> 00:36:02,236
Yeah.

532
00:36:02,260 --> 00:36:04,705
Yeah. There's something...

533
00:36:04,729 --> 00:36:06,941
Something doesn't quite follow.

534
00:36:06,965 --> 00:36:08,008
What?

535
00:36:08,032 --> 00:36:10,411
Well, the forensics
people estimated

536
00:36:10,435 --> 00:36:12,447
from the trajectory
of the bullet

537
00:36:12,471 --> 00:36:16,350
that the victim was
standing on this third step

538
00:36:16,374 --> 00:36:18,653
when he was hit. Right.

539
00:36:18,677 --> 00:36:22,290
But look at this
little trail of blood...

540
00:36:22,314 --> 00:36:24,681
from that cut on his right foot.

541
00:36:26,651 --> 00:36:29,530
There's blood here
on the landing...

542
00:36:29,554 --> 00:36:31,465
and on this first step,

543
00:36:31,489 --> 00:36:32,533
and none on the second.

544
00:36:32,557 --> 00:36:34,569
And if their
estimate is correct,

545
00:36:34,593 --> 00:36:37,126
there should be blood
on this step, but there isn't.

546
00:36:39,564 --> 00:36:40,930
Someone's in the kitchen.

547
00:36:46,571 --> 00:36:48,249
Oh. I'm sorry.

548
00:36:48,273 --> 00:36:49,450
We didn't mean to startle you.

549
00:36:49,474 --> 00:36:52,020
I'm Ben Matlock. This
is Michelle Thomas.

550
00:36:52,044 --> 00:36:54,121
We represent Andrea Morrow.

551
00:36:54,145 --> 00:36:57,058
Hattie Larson. Um,
Mr. Graham's housekeeper.

552
00:36:57,082 --> 00:37:00,094
I... I came here to clear out
my things and leave the key.

553
00:37:00,118 --> 00:37:01,696
Oh, you go ahead. Don't mind us.

554
00:37:01,720 --> 00:37:04,821
We're just... nosing around.

555
00:37:05,958 --> 00:37:07,023
Ben?

556
00:37:09,561 --> 00:37:11,372
Circuit breakers. Mm.

557
00:37:11,396 --> 00:37:14,564
Excuse me. Oh. I'm sorry.

558
00:37:21,273 --> 00:37:23,584
Miss Larson?

559
00:37:23,608 --> 00:37:27,421
Uh, how often did you come
in and clean for Mr. Graham?

560
00:37:27,445 --> 00:37:30,046
I came in at 10, left at
5, day in and day out.

561
00:37:31,316 --> 00:37:33,861
And how often did you
empty the wastebasket?

562
00:37:33,885 --> 00:37:35,062
Every day.

563
00:37:35,086 --> 00:37:37,598
So the last time
this one was emptied

564
00:37:37,622 --> 00:37:39,200
was the day he was murdered.

565
00:37:39,224 --> 00:37:40,423
That's right.

566
00:37:43,161 --> 00:37:46,062
I recall Arthur
Hampton to the stand.

567
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:51,060
Mr. Hampton...

568
00:37:51,770 --> 00:37:55,750
under... Under
what circumstances

569
00:37:55,774 --> 00:37:57,752
did you lose your sight?

570
00:37:57,776 --> 00:38:01,689
Uh, I suffered a
severe head injury

571
00:38:01,713 --> 00:38:04,124
uh, several years ago,
when the car I was riding in

572
00:38:04,148 --> 00:38:05,826
swerved off the road
and struck a tree.

573
00:38:05,850 --> 00:38:07,962
Who was driving the car?

574
00:38:07,986 --> 00:38:09,652
Clay was. Clay Graham.

575
00:38:11,156 --> 00:38:14,201
You know, that's a...
That's a horrible thing

576
00:38:14,225 --> 00:38:15,703
for a promising young artist

577
00:38:15,727 --> 00:38:18,039
to be robbed of
his sight like that.

578
00:38:18,063 --> 00:38:20,775
It must... It must've
made you pretty bitter.

579
00:38:20,799 --> 00:38:22,276
No, not really.

580
00:38:22,300 --> 00:38:24,712
If you'll forgive the
cliché, Mr. Matlock,

581
00:38:24,736 --> 00:38:27,247
I honestly think it made
me a better person.

582
00:38:27,271 --> 00:38:29,283
Perhaps, uh, I don't know...

583
00:38:29,307 --> 00:38:30,718
a better artist.

584
00:38:30,742 --> 00:38:32,519
P... Perhaps bitter's
the wrong word.

585
00:38:32,543 --> 00:38:35,723
But you did allow Clay Graham

586
00:38:35,747 --> 00:38:37,958
to do certain things
for you, didn't you?

587
00:38:37,982 --> 00:38:40,061
Like... Like buy
you that studio,

588
00:38:40,085 --> 00:38:41,729
pay the rent on your apartment,

589
00:38:41,753 --> 00:38:44,165
hire a part-time
secretary, things like that.

590
00:38:44,189 --> 00:38:47,001
Uh, I'm sure that under
the circumstances,

591
00:38:47,025 --> 00:38:49,737
Clay felt guilty about
what happened.

592
00:38:49,761 --> 00:38:51,739
But I prefer to
think that he acted,

593
00:38:51,763 --> 00:38:53,696
uh, in the main,
out of friendship.

594
00:38:55,100 --> 00:39:01,649
Uh, I... I suppose,
um... that friendship

595
00:39:01,673 --> 00:39:03,651
would explain

596
00:39:03,675 --> 00:39:07,121
why you spent so
much time at his house.

597
00:39:07,145 --> 00:39:10,091
I understand you'd come
over all... All by yourself.

598
00:39:10,115 --> 00:39:12,259
No seeing eye dog, no
one to guide you, nothing.

599
00:39:12,283 --> 00:39:13,394
Is that right? Yes.

600
00:39:13,418 --> 00:39:16,330
Well, I prefer not to
be a burden to anyone.

601
00:39:16,354 --> 00:39:20,267
I... I take great pride
in my independence.

602
00:39:20,291 --> 00:39:22,670
And I hear he even
gave you a key,

603
00:39:22,694 --> 00:39:25,039
so you could come
and go as you pleased.

604
00:39:25,063 --> 00:39:26,674
Yes.

605
00:39:26,698 --> 00:39:29,443
So you knew his
house pretty well.

606
00:39:29,467 --> 00:39:31,112
Yes.

607
00:39:31,136 --> 00:39:39,136
Um... Mr. Hampton...
do you own a gun?

608
00:39:39,477 --> 00:39:40,554
Yes.

609
00:39:40,578 --> 00:39:42,522
Do you know how to use it?

610
00:39:42,546 --> 00:39:43,579
Yes.

611
00:39:45,016 --> 00:39:49,063
Um... let me, uh...

612
00:39:49,087 --> 00:39:51,954
Let me tell you
something I think.

613
00:39:53,191 --> 00:39:56,737
I think that someone

614
00:39:56,761 --> 00:40:00,708
who knew Miss
Morrow and Mr. Graham

615
00:40:00,732 --> 00:40:03,744
were going to be
in that house alone...

616
00:40:03,768 --> 00:40:07,147
Someone with a key

617
00:40:07,171 --> 00:40:09,417
let himself in that house

618
00:40:09,441 --> 00:40:12,753
and rendered Miss
Morrow unconscious

619
00:40:12,777 --> 00:40:15,356
the instant she came
through the door,

620
00:40:15,380 --> 00:40:17,513
using some form of judo.

621
00:40:19,351 --> 00:40:21,495
You know judo, don't you?

622
00:40:21,519 --> 00:40:23,497
I know... a little.

623
00:40:23,521 --> 00:40:25,466
Now... Now, don't be
modest, Mr. Hampton.

624
00:40:25,490 --> 00:40:27,771
You were a Green
Beret, weren't you?

625
00:40:28,626 --> 00:40:29,736
Yes.

626
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:33,440
And you were
expert enough at judo

627
00:40:33,464 --> 00:40:35,642
to enter several competitions?

628
00:40:35,666 --> 00:40:36,777
Yes.

629
00:40:36,801 --> 00:40:39,780
So it is possible that you could

630
00:40:39,804 --> 00:40:43,417
render someone unconscious.

631
00:40:43,441 --> 00:40:46,587
It was true, yes.

632
00:40:46,611 --> 00:40:47,922
Years ago.

633
00:40:47,946 --> 00:40:49,256
Uh...

634
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:51,680
let me go ahead
with what I think here.

635
00:40:52,450 --> 00:40:57,732
I... I think... that
same someone,

636
00:40:57,756 --> 00:41:01,736
who rendered Miss
Morrow unconscious,

637
00:41:01,760 --> 00:41:07,541
placed a certain piece of paper

638
00:41:07,565 --> 00:41:12,112
on the third step from
the top of the stairs...

639
00:41:12,136 --> 00:41:14,315
then hit the
master light switch,

640
00:41:14,339 --> 00:41:16,450
turning out all of the lights,

641
00:41:16,474 --> 00:41:19,220
then took the gun
out of the drawer

642
00:41:19,244 --> 00:41:21,644
where Clay Graham kept it...

643
00:41:23,147 --> 00:41:29,630
then walked to the
bottom of the stairs...

644
00:41:29,654 --> 00:41:30,694
and waited.

645
00:41:32,490 --> 00:41:34,401
It's possible for a person to do

646
00:41:34,425 --> 00:41:35,736
all of that in
the dark, isn't it?

647
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:39,240
I mean, for someone as
familiar with the house as,

648
00:41:39,264 --> 00:41:40,863
say, you were.

649
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:44,211
Anything's possible.

650
00:41:44,235 --> 00:41:47,781
And then as Clay Graham
groped his way down those stairs,

651
00:41:47,805 --> 00:41:51,152
someone who knew how
to use a gun in the dark,

652
00:41:51,176 --> 00:41:52,586
such as yourself,

653
00:41:52,610 --> 00:41:56,189
shot him, and then
planted that gun

654
00:41:56,213 --> 00:41:58,692
in Miss Morrow's purse. No.

655
00:41:58,716 --> 00:42:02,462
And that same someone who
had ridden in Clay Graham's car

656
00:42:02,486 --> 00:42:04,164
hundreds of times...

657
00:42:04,188 --> 00:42:05,733
let himself in the car,

658
00:42:05,757 --> 00:42:08,001
picked up the car telephone

659
00:42:08,025 --> 00:42:09,737
and dialed the Graham house.

660
00:42:09,761 --> 00:42:11,060
Absolutely not.

661
00:42:16,768 --> 00:42:21,749
According... to the
mobile phone company,

662
00:42:21,773 --> 00:42:25,652
two calls were
placed from that phone

663
00:42:25,676 --> 00:42:28,589
after he was killed.

664
00:42:28,613 --> 00:42:30,257
That doesn't prove anything.

665
00:42:30,281 --> 00:42:33,627
One to the security company.

666
00:42:33,651 --> 00:42:35,811
Which shows how
they got there so fast.

667
00:42:36,788 --> 00:42:40,267
The second was
your call to Andrea,

668
00:42:40,291 --> 00:42:41,802
telling her to run.

669
00:42:41,826 --> 00:42:44,138
Mr. Matlock...

670
00:42:44,162 --> 00:42:46,774
I was nowhere near that place.

671
00:42:46,798 --> 00:42:48,776
I was at my
apartment that night.

672
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:50,733
<i>All</i> night.

673
00:42:57,475 --> 00:42:59,341
Uh...

674
00:43:03,381 --> 00:43:06,426
Your Honor, at this time...

675
00:43:06,450 --> 00:43:08,729
I'd like to enter into evidence

676
00:43:08,753 --> 00:43:10,998
Defense Exhibit G:

677
00:43:11,022 --> 00:43:13,734
this piece of paper.

678
00:43:13,758 --> 00:43:17,371
It was found in the wastebasket

679
00:43:17,395 --> 00:43:19,629
in the kitchen of
Clay Graham's home.

680
00:43:23,768 --> 00:43:27,415
Now, Mr. Graham's
housekeeper is certain

681
00:43:27,439 --> 00:43:30,217
that wastebasket was empty

682
00:43:30,241 --> 00:43:33,754
when she left the house at 5:00

683
00:43:33,778 --> 00:43:35,188
the day of the murder,

684
00:43:35,212 --> 00:43:38,158
which means...
this piece of paper

685
00:43:38,182 --> 00:43:41,717
had to be thrown
there by the killer.

686
00:43:43,988 --> 00:43:45,148
You know what's on it?

687
00:43:46,424 --> 00:43:47,868
I have no idea.

688
00:43:47,892 --> 00:43:49,191
Of course you don't.

689
00:43:51,195 --> 00:43:52,528
How could you know?

690
00:43:54,799 --> 00:43:57,778
How could you know...

691
00:43:57,802 --> 00:44:00,781
that Clay Graham would
break a glass that night

692
00:44:00,805 --> 00:44:03,384
and cut his right foot...

693
00:44:03,408 --> 00:44:05,519
leaving a little
trail of blood...

694
00:44:05,543 --> 00:44:10,257
along the hall...
down the stairs,

695
00:44:10,281 --> 00:44:13,382
ending on this piece of paper.

696
00:44:16,154 --> 00:44:18,165
Care to tell the court why?

697
00:44:18,189 --> 00:44:20,122
I have no idea.

698
00:44:23,261 --> 00:44:25,306
Clay Graham...

699
00:44:25,330 --> 00:44:27,741
stepping on this
piece of paper...

700
00:44:27,765 --> 00:44:32,680
was your cue... when
and where to fire.

701
00:44:32,704 --> 00:44:35,482
You had placed this...

702
00:44:35,506 --> 00:44:37,986
on the third step from the top.

703
00:44:39,511 --> 00:44:43,557
And when you heard
Clay Graham's foot...

704
00:44:43,581 --> 00:44:46,127
hit this paper, you shot him.

705
00:44:46,151 --> 00:44:48,762
Then you retrieved
the piece of paper

706
00:44:48,786 --> 00:44:50,719
and threw it away.

707
00:44:51,789 --> 00:44:53,767
How could you know...

708
00:44:53,791 --> 00:44:56,392
it had Clay
Graham's blood on it?

709
00:45:01,265 --> 00:45:04,111
Uh, I'm going to ask...

710
00:45:04,135 --> 00:45:08,648
the assistant district
attorney to read...

711
00:45:08,672 --> 00:45:10,484
what's printed on
this piece of paper.

712
00:45:18,649 --> 00:45:21,628
"Arthur Hampton Studios,

713
00:45:21,652 --> 00:45:25,399
"947 Temple Street,
Atlanta, Georgia.

714
00:45:37,235 --> 00:45:38,267
I'm sorry.

715
00:45:39,636 --> 00:45:41,756
I'm sorry, Mr. Hampton. I...

716
00:45:44,275 --> 00:45:47,387
I don't... understand...

717
00:45:47,411 --> 00:45:49,556
the world of the sightless.

718
00:45:49,580 --> 00:45:51,558
I don't know.

719
00:45:51,582 --> 00:45:53,942
You'd planned
everything so carefully.

720
00:45:55,286 --> 00:45:57,431
I guess you thought this was

721
00:45:57,455 --> 00:45:59,655
a blank piece of drawing paper.

722
00:46:03,427 --> 00:46:05,472
Nothing further.

723
00:46:05,496 --> 00:46:07,374
State moves for a
dismissal, Your Honor.

724
00:46:13,371 --> 00:46:14,982
Oh, boy. Yeah. Ha-ha.

725
00:46:15,006 --> 00:46:17,384
Howdy, Cassie. Hi, Cassie.

726
00:46:17,408 --> 00:46:18,441
Oh-ha.

727
00:46:19,544 --> 00:46:21,722
Oh.

728
00:46:21,746 --> 00:46:24,958
Oh, what's...
What's all this now?

729
00:46:24,982 --> 00:46:27,461
Well, Ben, you needed a new suit

730
00:46:27,485 --> 00:46:29,330
for the Bar Association
dinner next week.

731
00:46:29,354 --> 00:46:31,565
You are the keynote speaker.

732
00:46:31,589 --> 00:46:33,567
Well, what's
wrong with this suit?

733
00:46:33,591 --> 00:46:36,670
Don't you think it's
getting a little threadbare?

734
00:46:36,694 --> 00:46:37,694
I do.

735
00:46:40,464 --> 00:46:43,110
I'm... I'm sorry,
but could you...

736
00:46:43,134 --> 00:46:44,978
Could you take it back?

737
00:46:45,002 --> 00:46:46,646
Look, after all the trouble

738
00:46:46,670 --> 00:46:47,981
we went through
of picking it out,

739
00:46:48,005 --> 00:46:50,485
the least you
can do is look at it.

740
00:46:52,577 --> 00:46:55,017
It's from all of us.

741
00:46:55,246 --> 00:46:56,991
With love.

742
00:47:09,093 --> 00:47:10,133
Oh.

743
00:47:11,229 --> 00:47:12,389
Oh.

744
00:47:13,431 --> 00:47:14,741
Now, that's a suit.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
